course: dictionary
lesson: 9185
title: Soccer or football?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name different things in different places. The clues around the word show you which meaning is meant. «A boy in London tells his friend in Chicago that he loves football, and the friend imagines helmets and touchdowns, until they work out the boy means soccer.» The friend's picture of helmets and touchdowns is American football, so the clue shows the boy means the game with a round ball.
explain: Soccer is a game played by two teams of eleven players who kick a round ball and try to score goals. Only the goalkeeper may use their hands. Everyone else must keep the ball away from their hands.
explain: In the USA, Canada, Australia and Ireland the game is often called soccer. In the UK and much of the world it is called football. The word means the same game, so check the place and the words around it.
explain: Safety matters too. «Never climb on or hang from a soccer goal; unanchored goals can fall and crush children, so tell an adult if one wobbles.»
fact: Soccer is a noun. It is the name of a game, and you say play soccer.
fact: «Soccer» means a game played by two teams of eleven players who kick a round ball and try to score goals.
fact: In soccer, only the goalkeeper may use their hands.
fact: «Football» means different games in different places: soccer in the UK, Ireland and most of the world, and American football in the USA and Canada.
fact: A soccer goal must be anchored so it cannot tip over and hurt someone.
exercise: «The coach told us to play soccer after lunch.» What is soccer? | *A game for two teams of eleven players who kick a round ball | A race around a track with batons | A game where players run with an oval ball and wear helmets | A type of bat used for cricket
exercise: In soccer, which players may use their hands? | *Only the goalkeeper | Nobody, hands are never allowed in any position | Only the players in the middle of the field | Any player who wants to
exercise: Find and copy the word that names a game: «Our soccer team practises on the field every Tuesday.» | *soccer | Tuesday | team | field
test: In the UK, what does «football» usually mean? | *Soccer, the game with a round ball | American football with helmets and touchdowns | Rugby league with a tall pole
test: Why should you tell an adult if a soccer goal wobbles? | *It can fall over and crush a child | It might be painted the wrong colour | Goals always make a loud noise | Wobbly goals score more points
exam: When you meet a word with two meanings, what is a good check? | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Ask someone to change the word for you | Ignore the sentence and guess quickly | Look up the word in another book
